Replace src/patch_generator.py with a tool-using patch pipeline. It runs in the final phase, only on confirmed PoV-backed crashes, behind env flag DISCVER_GENERATE_PATCHES (default OFF). For each such crash it tries to produce a fix that (1) stops the crash and (2) doesn't break existing tests, then writes the fix out for a human to apply. Never emit a fix that hasn't passed both checks.

Build it as a loop of small steps, each a separate call to VLLMClient.chat, and give every step the SAME code-navigation tools that agent_tools.py already exposes (read_file, grep, list_files) so the model can look things up in the real source itself instead of being handed a big pre-built context string. This matters: fixes usually fail to compile because the model couldn't see a type definition, a helper, or a caller it depended on — so the model must be able to grep/read for those itself.

STEP 1 — understand the bug. Input: the crash trace/ASAN output, the PoV input, the sink file:line. The model uses read_file to open the crashing function, then uses grep/read_file to pull in what that code depends on — the types it uses, helper functions it calls, and who calls it. Output: a short structured root-cause description (what the bug is, where, why it triggers).

STEP 2 — decide the fix. From the root cause, the model states the minimal change that fixes the underlying cause (not just the crash site). It may span multiple locations/files if the root cause does. It must preserve existing behavior — no functional changes beyond closing the vulnerability.

STEP 3 — write the fix. The model produces the complete edited contents of each file it changes (full file, not a diff — so we can render side-by-side later).

STEP 4 — self-check. The model re-reads its own edited files against the root cause and confirms the fix addresses it and doesn't obviously break the file (syntax, removed logic). If it finds a problem, loop back to step 3.

VALIDATE (the gate — code, not LLM): take the edited files, copy the source tree, apply them, REBUILD the target the same way discver builds it for fuzzing, then check BOTH:
  (a) re-run the PoV input against the rebuilt target — the crash/sanitizer must NOT trigger.
  (b) run the project's existing test/build the same way discver already does — it must still pass.
If the build fails or either check fails, capture the exact stderr/test output, feed it back to the model as a new step ("your patch produced this error, fix it"), and retry from step 3. Cap at 3 total attempts. If still failing after 3, record failure and emit nothing for this crash.

SECOND STRATEGY (separate flag DISCVER_PATCH_CODING_AGENT, default OFF): if the loop above fails all 3 attempts, run a fuller free-form attempt — give the model an open read/grep/edit loop over a copy of the repo (more turns, less structure) and let it iterate toward a fix, then run the SAME validation gate on its result. Take whichever strategy first produces a patch that passes validation. If this flag is off or its backend isn't available, skip it cleanly — strategy 1 alone must still work.

EMIT (only after validation passes): write to output/patches/<crash-id>/ the full patched file(s) AND the original file(s) side-by-side, plus PATCH_NOTES.md containing: the vulnerability, the root cause, what changed and why, which strategy produced it, and the validation result (PoV neutralized, tests pass). Do NOT modify the real source tree — a human applies it manually.

RECORD via the discver.reliability logger and the run report, per crash: patch produced (which strategy) / failed after retries / skipped — so we can measure patch success rate.

